WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.199
If you're building high-stakes agentic pipelines, programmatic validation layers are what make

2
00:00:05.199 --> 00:00:11.119
probabilistic LLM outputs reliable. In this lesson, we'll walk through pre-execution,

3
00:00:11.119 --> 00:00:17.520
post-execution, and runtime check patterns. We'll look hard at block versus soft warning response

4
00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:23.520
modes and examine how to balance enforcement strictness with the flexibility that real

5
00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:30.159
workflows require. Let's get into it. Every mechanism in this lesson exists because code

6
00:00:30.159 --> 00:00:35.200
can hold a line. The model can't. You already know the validation gate, the checkpoint that

7
00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:41.279
inspects a step's output before execution continues. What this lesson adds is where you

8
00:00:41.279 --> 00:00:47.759
put one and what it does when the check fails. An escalation trigger is the specific condition

9
00:00:47.759 --> 00:00:53.599
that routes an agent run to human review, and it has to be calibrated because too sensitive

10
00:00:53.599 --> 00:00:59.680
produces constant friction and too permissive misses the cases that actually need a person.

11
00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:04.559
Programmatic enforcement is the code layer substrate underneath both schema checks,

12
00:01:04.559 --> 00:01:09.680
policy engines, middleware interceptors, mechanisms that hold regardless of what

13
00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:14.400
the model outputs. The model's compliance probability stops mattering once code is

14
00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:21.599
doing the check. Pre-execution validation is the first code layer gate. It runs before

15
00:01:21.599 --> 00:01:27.440
the agent takes any action and checks whether the inputs are valid. Schema validation confirms

16
00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:33.680
that the incoming request matches the expected data structure and field types. Range checks

17
00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:39.360
verify that numeric parameters are within allowed bounds. Permission verification confirms that the

18
00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:45.839
requesting caller is actually authorized to trigger this action. These checks are inexpensive

19
00:01:45.839 --> 00:01:52.720
and run fast because they evaluate structured data rather than model outputs. Stopping an incorrect

20
00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:58.480
input at the pre-execution gate costs almost nothing. Catching the error after an incorrect

21
00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:04.639
action has already executed is far more expensive and in some cases impossible to reverse.

22
00:02:05.599 --> 00:02:11.839
Post-execution validation runs after the agent produces output but before that output is committed

23
00:02:11.839 --> 00:02:18.160
to storage or forwarded to a downstream system. Output schema compliance checks that the response

24
00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:23.839
matches the required structure, not just that it contains words. Business rule checks verify that

25
00:02:23.839 --> 00:02:29.600
the output satisfies domain-specific constraints that couldn't be evaluated on inputs alone.

26
00:02:30.559 --> 00:02:35.919
Anomaly detection flags outputs that are structurally valid but semantically unusual,

27
00:02:35.919 --> 00:02:40.960
for example a transaction amount that's within the allowed range but far outside the historical

28
00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:47.839
distribution. Post-execution gates catch model errors that pre-execution checks can't see because

29
00:02:47.839 --> 00:02:53.759
those errors only manifest in the generated output. Those gate types are complements not substitutes.

30
00:02:53.759 --> 00:02:59.360
You need both. The pre-execution and post-execution gates serve different functions

31
00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:05.360
and catch different failure modes. A pre-execution gate runs before the agent acts. It's the cheapest

32
00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:11.440
enforcement layer because it stops invalid inputs before any computation is done. It catches

33
00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:17.600
malformed requests, out-of-range parameters, unauthorized callers. A post-execution gate

34
00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:23.279
runs after the agent generates output but before that output causes any downstream effect.

35
00:03:23.279 --> 00:03:30.000
It's the safety net for errors that only become visible in the output. Schema violations the input

36
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:36.240
check couldn't anticipate. Business rule failures that depend on what the model chose to produce.

37
00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:42.639
Semantic anomalies that require looking at the full response. Running both in sequence is the

38
00:03:42.639 --> 00:03:49.839
standard pattern for any pipeline where output errors have real-world consequences. Validation

39
00:03:49.839 --> 00:03:55.279
gates need distinct response modes depending on the constraint they're enforcing. A hard block

40
00:03:55.279 --> 00:04:01.199
rejects the output and halts or retries the step. Hard blocks are appropriate for

41
00:04:01.199 --> 00:04:06.000
non-negotiable constraints like financial authorization limits, permission violations,

42
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:11.759
and safety boundaries. When the constraint is a hard block there is no path forward that includes

43
00:04:11.759 --> 00:04:18.320
the incorrect output. A soft warning flags the output and logs the issue but allows execution

44
00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:24.640
to continue which fits advisory constraints where the concern is real but a human reviewer can make

45
00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:30.799
the final call. An escalation trigger sits between them pausing the run and routing to a person when

46
00:04:30.799 --> 00:04:36.480
the case is genuinely ambiguous. The design decision is matching the response to the actual risk.

47
00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:42.480
Runtime checks are the enforcement layer that monitors the agent while it's running

48
00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:48.720
rather than at defined input or output boundaries. Cost limits enforce token budgets

49
00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:56.239
and API spend caps mid-run halting or throttling the agent before a runaway process causes a large

50
00:04:56.239 --> 00:05:02.640
unexpected bill. Iteration caps set a maximum number of loop cycles to prevent an agent from

51
00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:07.519
getting stuck in a reasoning loop that never terminates. Scope enforcement checks that the

52
00:05:07.519 --> 00:05:14.000
agent is only accessing the resources and data it's authorized to use. Flagging or blocking

53
00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:20.320
out-of-bounds access mid-execution rather than catching it in a post-execution review.

54
00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:26.000
Runtime checks are the safety net for failure modes that don't have a clean

55
00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:32.880
pre- or post-execution boundary. Resource overruns, emergent behaviors, and gradual scope creep.

56
00:05:33.679 --> 00:05:38.720
Layered enforcement works because each layer catches failures the layer before it missed.

57
00:05:39.279 --> 00:05:43.760
The prompt layer provides behavioral guidance at the lowest implementation cost.

58
00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:49.440
It's effective for reducing the frequency of non-compliant outputs, but it's probabilistic.

59
00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:56.000
The pre-execution layer adds schema, range, and permission checks before any action is taken.

60
00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:02.160
It catches invalid inputs before they reach the model. The post-execution layer validates outputs

61
00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:08.880
against schema requirements, business rules, and anomaly thresholds before results propagate

62
00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:15.679
downstream. The runtime layer monitors the agent continuously for cost, scope, and anomaly signals

63
00:06:15.679 --> 00:06:21.519
throughout execution. Together, these layers create a defense-in-depth architecture where

64
00:06:21.519 --> 00:06:27.040
no single point of failure produces a system failure. That's the design principle behind

65
00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:32.799
layered enforcement. Calibrating enforcement strictness is as important as implementing the

66
00:06:32.799 --> 00:06:38.399
layers themselves. Over-strict enforcement creates friction that breaks legitimate workflows

67
00:06:38.399 --> 00:06:43.200
and erodes trust in the system. Hard blocks should be reserved for constraints that are

68
00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:48.559
genuinely non-negotiable. Safety limits, compliance requirements, authorization boundaries.

69
00:06:49.119 --> 00:06:54.640
Soft warnings are appropriate for constraints that are advisory. Flagging unusual patterns,

70
00:06:54.640 --> 00:07:00.880
surfacing edge cases, logging outputs for review. Escalation triggers belong on the cases that are

71
00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:07.200
genuinely ambiguous. Situations where the right decision requires human judgment and context the

72
00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:13.119
agent doesn't have. The goal of calibration is matching the enforcement response to the actual

73
00:07:13.119 --> 00:07:20.000
risk level. A hard block that fires on a legitimate transaction is a system failure of a different kind

74
00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:25.760
than the one the block is meant to prevent. The failure you see in production is an agent that

75
00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:31.760
ran the whole chain and only surfaced the problem after the damage was built. A check before

76
00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:38.720
execution stops the bad input. A check after execution catches what only shows up in the result.

77
00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:45.600
Runtime limits catch the slow drift that has no clean boundary. Placement is the difference between

78
00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:51.760
catching a fault and reporting one later. That closes out this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

